%% arrangement-sequence.mmd — what happens, in order, when a line is typed into
%% the location bar.  The companion to `arrangement-dataflow.dot': that figure
%% says WHAT moves, this one says WHEN, and the participant boxes below are the
%% same four domains in the same order.
%%
%% The claim this drawing is for: ONE CHANNEL, WHATEVER IT IS ADDRESSING, AND
%% THE CLASS IS READ OFF THE PATCH RATHER THAN BELIEVED.
%%
%% Three things are easier to see here than in the dataflow figure:
%%
%%   * THE GRAMMAR DECIDES WITHOUT A MODEL, and it decides first.  Misreading a
%%     line is not symmetric: a path sent to a model spends money on a 404, a
%%     sentence read as a path produces a 404 and loses the sentence.  So the
%%     three branches are settled by `parseInstruction' before anything is
%%     asked of anyone.
%%
%%   * THE MODEL IS ASKED FOR A PATCH AND FOR NOTHING ELSE.  The adapter on
%%     this path is built with allowTools = [] and maxIterations = 1: there is
%%     no line to the store while the question is being asked, so the whole
%%     left-to-right traffic of the exploration figure is absent here by
%%     construction, not by luck.
%%
%%   * THE FORK IS DELIBERATE AND ASYMMETRIC.  A non-presentational patch is
%%     REFUSED on the workspace and GATED on a presentation.  The chrome holds
%%     no data, so a gate there would be asking a reader to authorise a query
%%     against a repository the workspace never names.
%%
%% And one asymmetry the figure does not hide: the two addressable kinds are
%% not persisted the same way.  The workspace is a tree of triples in
%% `<account>/ui', one graph per presentation, written as a single four-
%% statement update.  A presentation's revision is recorded as an mcp:Dashboard
%% JSON literal in `<account>/mcp' by the same `recordRun' the exploration uses.
%%
%% Source: lib/app/instructions.js (parseInstruction, target), ui/app.js
%% (instructWorkspace, addressableTargets, redrawChrome), lib/app/workspace-
%% instruction.js (runWorkspaceInstruction), lib/app/patch.js (applyPatch,
%% classify), lib/app/ui-store.js (writePresentation), ui/components/
%% dashboard-pane.js (startRevision, receiveRevision, presentChange,
%% applyRevision, record).

sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  actor U as you

  box rgb(253,247,241) the main document — one instruction channel, whatever it is addressing
    participant B as the location bar
    participant A as THE ADDRESS
    participant X as apply · classify
  end
  box rgb(250,236,226) the model provider — asked for a patch, and for nothing else
    participant M as the model
  end
  box rgb(228,238,250) the dydra service
    participant W as account/ui
    participant R as account/mcp
    participant S as the repository
  end
  box rgb(238,238,238) the page frame — opaque origin, no credential, no network
    participant F as the page
  end

  U->>B: a line
  B->>B: parseInstruction — tiny, and decidable without a model

  alt the line is a path, with or without goto
    B->>B: renderPage
    B-->>U: the page
  else goto name
    B->>A: re-address
    alt it names one thing
      A-->>B: the workspace, or that presentation
      B-->>U: the target indicator changes, and nothing else happens
    else it names none, or more than one
      A-->>B: REFUSED — never the nearest match
      Note over B,A: what a wrong guess re-addresses is what the NEXT<br/>sentence will change, so a near miss is not a kindness
    end
  else anything else — an instruction, against the address shown
    B->>A: what is addressed, and its document
    A-->>B: the contract for that kind, and the document as an outline
    Note over B,X: a presentation has panels, queries, parameters and a grant.<br/>The chrome has none, so it gets the shorter contract.  Offer a model a<br/>vocabulary of queries for a surface that cannot run one, and the cheapest<br/>way to satisfy a request becomes writing one into the furniture

    B->>M: the instruction, the contract, and the outline<br/>· JSON + the PROVIDER key
    Note right of M: allowTools = [] and maxIterations = 1 —<br/>no line to the store while asking
    M-->>B: a patch · JSON ops

    alt the answer carried no patch block
      B-->>U: nothing has been changed — and if it was cut off at the<br/>token ceiling with the block still open, ask for a smaller change
    else a patch
      B->>X: applyPatch against a COPY of the document
      alt any operation is invalid
        X-->>B: refused whole — nothing was half-applied
        B-->>U: the errors, and the patch, to read
      else applied
        X->>X: classify before against after — read off the documents,<br/>not off what the patch said it was doing

        alt the address is the WORKSPACE and the class is not presentational
          X-->>B: REFUSED — the interface itself holds no data
          Note over X: the model has written a panel or a parameter into<br/>the furniture.  A gate here would ask for consent to<br/>a query against a repository the workspace never names
        else a presentation, class = authority
          X-->>B: REFUSED — a patch cannot widen a grant.<br/>Close the page and start again with the authority you want to give
        else a presentation, class = data or computational
          X-->>U: GATE — the panels before and after, side by side
          U->>X: Run n panels and apply
          X->>S: runPanels — only the panels the classifier says changed<br/>· SPARQL + the user's token
          S-->>X: RESULT JSON
        else presentational
          Note over X: applied without a gate — a change of colour that asked<br/>permission would teach the reader to stop reading the<br/>gates that matter
        end

        alt the workspace
          X->>W: DROP SILENT GRAPH, INSERT DATA, DELETE WHERE, INSERT DATA<br/>— one update, one graph
          W-->>X: revision n+1
          X->>B: redrawChrome — the whole page, not the chrome alone:<br/>a band that moved takes its contents with it
        else a presentation
          X->>F: the change, as instructions · postMessage
          F-->>X: in place, or rebuilt
          Note over X: in place, because rebuilding would throw away the scroll<br/>position, the open tab, a selection and the parameter just<br/>chosen — making the cheap change feel worse than the expensive one
          X->>R: recordRun, disposition "revised" · SPARQL update
        end

        X-->>B: what the patch did, in a line
        B-->>U: the reply, with an UNDO beside it

        opt the reader presses undo
          U->>B: undo
          B->>X: the document as it was, held from before the write
          Note over X: an authority change cannot be undone here — it needed a grant
          X->>X: put back through the same path the change took, so going<br/>backwards over a presentational change re-runs nothing
          alt the workspace
            X->>W: written again
          else a presentation
            X->>R: recorded again, disposition "revised"
          end
          Note over X,W: a revert is a NEW revision and not a rewind — whoever has<br/>already read the version being undone still has something that happened
          B-->>U: put back
        end
      end
    end
  end
